cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this work, please cite it as below."
title: "A linearly stable excited dark-matter core, proved across its window up to 0.002-wide edge brackets: certified spectral stability of the one-node Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson state for every central amplitude in [1597/1000, 1983/1000], and certified brackets of its stability-band edges"
authors:
  - name: "Moki & Julio"
version: "2.0"
doi: "10.5281/zenodo.24359032"
license: CC-BY-4.0
keywords:
  - dark matter
  - ultralight dark matter
  - boson stars
  - Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson
  - Schrodinger-Poisson
  - excited states
  - spectral stability
  - Krein signature
  - Hamiltonian-Krein index
  - computer-assisted proof
  - interval arithmetic
  - rigorous numerics
abstract: >-
  For the repulsive Gross-Pitaevskii-Poisson system in the units of arXiv:2402.07998, we prove with interval (ball)
  arithmetic that the first excited (one-node) stationary state is spectrally stable for every central amplitude
  sigma0 in [1597/1000, 1983/1000] (version 2.0): there is a decaying one-node solution for each sigma0, continuous in
  sigma0, equal to the certified state at 23 rational nodes and unique in an explicit ball around a cubic Hermite
  predictor, and the linearised operator J L on L^2(R^3) has no spectrum with Re lambda > 0 for any of them. The proof
  certifies drops in the inertia of L + i w J between two frequencies (the classical local Krein index identity, used
  as a computer-assisted certificate), makes them uniform on 22 cells by a convexity cover with exact quadratic tests,
  and encloses the branch between nodes by a Newton-Kantorovich argument; the sealed test held on 22 of 22 cells,
  23 of 23 nodes and 16 of 16 controls (one control rule set after seeing its data, disclosed). Version 1.0 proved
  stability at sigma0 = 9/5 (E = [-3.10544025447 +/- 3.02e-12]; n(L+) = (2, 1, 1, 0), n(L-) = (1, 1, 1, 0) for
  L = 0..3; negative-Krein eigenvalues certified at L = 0, 1, 2) and at eight more points, certified linear
  instability at 1595/1000, 31/20, 2 and 1985/1000, and mapped the float band [1.5963117, 1.9841288], whose edges are
  Krein collisions and lie in the certified brackets [1.595, 1.597] and [1.983, 1.985]. Proved, computer-assisted,
  internally AI-refereed; not checked in a proof assistant. Not claimed: nonlinear or orbital stability, uniqueness
  beyond the stated ball, the exact edge points, any astrophysical statement.
